cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset, please cite both the dataset and the article it accompanies (see preferred-citation)."
title: "clinical-rag-neurophysio-data: research data for the empirical quantification of the benefit of Retrieval-Augmented Generation in a lightweight on-premise clinical assistant for neurophysiotherapy in Spanish"
type: dataset
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: '2026-07-11'
# Two licences: the data are CC BY 4.0 (see LICENSE); the code under code/ is MIT
# (see code/LICENSE-CODE). NOTICE.md is the authoritative statement of the scope of each.
license:
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/UM-Ruben/clinical-rag-neurophysio-data"
authors:
  - family-names: "Fernández García"
    given-names: "Rubén"
    affiliation: "Universidad de Murcia"
    email: ruben.fernandezg@um.es
  - family-names: "García Carrasco"
    given-names: "José Manuel"
    affiliation: "Universidad de Murcia"
    email: jmgarcia@um.es
abstract: >-
  Research data accompanying the article "Las Bondades del RAG" (The Benefits of RAG),
  an empirical quantification of the benefit of context retrieval in a lightweight,
  private, on-premise clinical assistant for neurophysiotherapy in Spanish. The
  repository publishes the raw inference records of two experiments over four locally
  deployable 7B-8B language models. Study P1 (ablation) contrasts each model with and
  without RAG on a 53-item gold-standard question bank, three options per item and
  abstention forbidden by design, for a total of 424 inferences. Study P2
  (hallucination) permits abstention through a fourth option and evaluates the same
  four models, with and without RAG, over three banks (the 53 answerable items, 24
  TRAP items carrying a false premise in the stem, and 18 out-of-distribution items
  that the corpus cannot answer), for a total of 760 inferences. Also published are
  the three question banks, an adjudicated taxonomy of the 131 erroneous responses of
  Study P1 across five mutually exclusive categories, a blind error-detectability
  panel with three LLM judges (two weak local models and one frontier model), the
  retrieval provenance of the 368 chunks retrieved for the 53 answerable questions,
  and the pre-computed aggregates behind every figure reported in the article. There
  is no independent human annotator: the frontier judge produced the taxonomy and
  detectability labels, and the author reviewed and endorsed them without
  modification (131 of 131 categories unchanged), so no human-machine agreement is
  published or computable. Accuracies of the two protocols are not comparable with
  each other, as the number of options and the abstention policy differ. The source
  code is published under code/ (MIT licence, distinct from the CC BY 4.0 of the
  data): an analysis layer that regenerates every regenerable aggregate from the
  published data alone, verifiable with a single command (python code/reproduce.py,
  exit 0 when everything matches), and an inference layer published for inspection,
  which requires the corpus and a local Ollama server. The teaching corpus is NOT
  published (third-party copyright), and neither is the QLoRA fine-tuning pipeline,
  whose training data derives from that corpus; consequently the QLoRA arm of the
  study is not reproducible by third parties, while the other three models
  (llama3.1:8b, qwen2.5:7b, thewindmom/llama3-med42-8b, all public on Ollama) are.
  The retrieved passages of Study P2 are released as SHA-256 digests so that a holder
  of a licensed copy of the corpus can verify the retrieval without the corpus being
  redistributed.
keywords:
  - RAG
  - retrieval-augmented generation
  - hallucination
  - clinical NLP
  - Spanish
  - neurorehabilitation
  - LLM evaluation
  - reproducibility
